course: dictionary
lesson: 13678
title: Inherently: built in from the start
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Inherently» means by its very nature. It describes something that is a basic, natural part of a thing and cannot be taken away. Think of it as "built in".
explain: «Fire is inherently dangerous, so we keep it in a fireplace.» Fire is dangerous in itself. Nobody has to add danger to it, because heat and flames are part of what fire is.
explain: Try swapping in a simpler word. If «Fire is naturally dangerous» still makes sense, you have found the right meaning. Inherently is a little more formal, so you will often find it in books, news and school science.
fact: Inherently means by its very nature, or in itself.
fact: «Inherently» is an adverb, so it tells you how something is or how something happens.
fact: «Some sports are inherently risky, so players wear helmets.» The risk comes from the sport itself.
fact: Inherently is often used in phrases like inherently dangerous, inherently good and inherently unfair.
fact: Something that is inherently true is true because of what it is, not because of something added from outside.
exercise: «Fire is inherently dangerous, so we keep it in a fireplace.» What does inherently mean here? | Dangerous only when someone adds fuel | *Dangerous by its very nature | Warm and cosy | Hard to find
exercise: «Some sports are inherently risky, so players wear helmets.» What does this sentence tell you about some sports? | They are only risky on wet days | They are fun to watch | They are never risky | *Their risk is part of the sport itself
exercise: Ben says a sweet is inherently sweet. What does he mean? | It is sweet because someone added sugar later | *It is sweet by its very nature | It is sweet only in summer | It is not sweet at all
test: «Some problems are inherently hard, even for adults.» What does inherently show? | The problem is written in a different language | *The difficulty is part of the problem itself | The problem became hard because of a mistake | The problem is easy to solve
test: Which word is closest in meaning to inherently? | Suddenly | Rarely | Quietly | *Naturally
exam: Which sentence uses inherently correctly? | Water inherently ran down the hill after the rain | I inherently ate my lunch at noon | The cat is inherently under the table | *Water is inherently wet, because wetness is part of what water is
